.Dd October 3, 2026
.Dt RCGC 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rcgc
.Nd remove leftover broken symlinks
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm rcgc
.Op Fl afhnqVv
.Op Fl d Ar dir
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This program removes symlinks created by
.Xr rcup 1
that no longer resolve.
.Pp
By default,
.Nm
will print the list of broken symlinks, then prompt for a confirmation to remove
them all at once. This behavior can be adjusted with the
.Fl f
and
.Fl n
flags.
.Pp
For performance,
.Nm
does not scan all of
.Pa ~
by default. See
.Sx SCAN SCOPE .
.Pp
If any empty directories are left over after removing broken symlinks, they are
also removed. (But a directory at the top of
.Pa ~ ,
such as
.Pa ~/.config ,
is always retained.)
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "-d DIR"
.It Fl a
scan all of
.Pa ~
rather than only the subdirectories that
.Xr rcup 1
could create. See
.Sx SCAN SCOPE .
.It Fl d Ar DIR
remove links pointing into
.Ar DIR
instead of those named by
.Va DOTFILES_DIRS .
This can be specified multiple times.
.It Fl f
do not prompt to delete links; assume a response of
.Qq y .
.It Fl h
show usage instructions.
.It Fl n
do not prompt to delete links; assume a response of
.Qq n .
.It Fl q
decrease verbosity. Implies
.Fl n.
Use with
.Fl f
to remove links quietly.
.It Fl v
increase verbosity. This can be repeated for extra verbosity.
.It Fl V
show the version number.
.El
.
.Sh SCAN SCOPE
In principle, a stale symlink could be anywhere in the home directory.
However, walking all of
.Pa ~
on each invocation of
.Nm
would be, in general, very inefficient. Home directories can be large, and
dotfiles are usually only kept in a few places. Thus, by default,
.Nm
uses a heuristic to optimize performance at the cost of correctness, leading to
false-negatives (under-reported garbage) in certain cases. To disable the
heuristic and scan all of
.Pa ~ ,
pass
.Fl a .
.Ss HEURISTIC
.Nm
assumes two things about where dotfiles are located: (1) the set of top-level
subdirectories of
.Pa ~
that might have ever contained dotfiles is fairly static, and (2) everything in
these directories is a dotfile. For example, on most systems, all dotfiles
either sit directly within
.Pa ~
(e.g.
.Pa ~/.bashrc ) ,
or within
.Pa ~/.config ,
which only contains dotfiles.
Under these assumptions, the only recursive directory scans that
.Nm
needs to perform in order to find stale symlinks are for those top-level
subdirectories of
.Pa ~
that are
.Qq implied
by the layout of the dotfiles directories as they currently
exist. A directory is
.Qq implied
when, under any possible values of
.Va TAGS
and hostname,
.Xr rcup 1
would create that directory if it did not already exist (the actual configured
hostname and tags are ignored by
.Nm
because a broken link might be the result of changing this configuration for the
current host).
.Pp
This heuristic produces false negatives when assumption (1) is false--that is,
when one of these top-level directories is no longer
.Qq implied
by the set of rcm dotfiles directories, either because it has been deleted or
renamed, or because the host's tags or hostname has changed in the right (wrong)
way. For example, if the
.Qq destination
directory
.Pa ~/bar
is only implied by the
.Qq source
directory
.Pa tag-foo/bar/ ,
then deleting the source directory (or removing the
.Qq foo
tag from the configuration) will leave behind broken symlinks that
.Nm
will not see unless the heuristic is disabled with
.Fl a .
.Ss EXCLUDING LARGE DIRECTORIES
When assumption (2) is false, the heuristic will perform deep scans of
directories that mostly do not contain dotfiles, which hurts performance. For
example, on macOS,
.Pa ~/Library
holds thousands of system- and application-owned files, but
.Pa ~/Library/LaunchAgents
can contain launchd plist files that might be managed with rcm. Scanning all of
.Pa ~/Library
on each
.Nm
invocation would be wasteful, as most directories within it will never contain
rcm-created symlinks.
.Pp
In such a situation, it would be more performant to give these large top-level
directories the same treatment as
.Pa ~ :
scan shallowly, then scan any implied subdirectories deeply.
.Nm
cannot know on its own which directories warrant this type of scan, but it can
be informed via an rcrc setting: use
.Va GC_SHALLOW_DIRS
to declare which dotfile-containing directories should be scanned shallowly
rather than deeply. In the macOS example, declaring
.Dl GC_SHALLOW_DIRS="Library"
will tell
.Nm
to scan
.Pa ~/Library
shallowly, preventing wasteful scanning; but
.Pa ~/Library/LaunchAgents ,
because it is implied by the dotfiles directories, will still be scanned deeply.
This can of course lead to false negatives just like before: if
.Pa ~/Library/LaunchAgents
stops being implied, it will stop being scanned. Thus, this feature should only
be used on directories where an assumption similar to (1) applies.
.Pp
An implied child can itself be a large directory of mostly non-dotfiles, so one
level of shallow scanning is not always enough. For example, one might have a
dotfile within
.Pa ~/Library/Application Support/Code ,
but scanning all of
.Pa ~/Library/Application Support
would be wasteful. In this case, declaring
.Dl GC_SHALLOW_DIRS="Library/Application?Support"
will tell
.Nm
to scan both
.Pa ~/Library
and
.Pa ~/Library/Application Support
shallowly, and
.Pa ~/Library/Application Support/Code
will be scanned deeply. That is, all ancestors of a shallowly-scanned directory
are also scanned shallowly (up to
.Pa ~ ) .
(A
.Qq \&?
must be used to match a space; see section
.Sx BUGS
in
.Xr rcrc 5 . )
.Pp
The items passed to
.Va GC_SHALLOW_DIRS
are interpreted as globs to be matched against the contents of the dotfiles
directories. They are not paths (e.g., it would be incorrect to pass
.Pa ~/Library ) ,
and they are not matched against the contents of
.Pa ~
(thus a leading dot must be omitted when naming a hidden directory).
because
.Nm
cleans broken symlinks for all dotfiles directories, these globs cannot be
scoped to a particular dotfiles directory--that is, they are not the
.Qq exclude patterns
that other rcm variables use. Note that the glob
.Qq *
character will match against
.Qq /
characters, so it should be used with care (this is also true of exclude
patterns).
.Pp
.Va GC_SHALLOW_DIRS
is ignored when
.Fl a
is passed.
.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
Because
.Xr rcup 1
cannot create a link whose target path contains a newline, or a link with a
relative target path, these links are always left alone even if they are broken,
as they are assumed to have been created manually.
.Pp
Dotfiles that were copied rather than symlinked into their destination,
because of
.Va COPY_ALWAYS ,
must be deleted by hand when their source is removed. Neither
.Nm
nor
.Xr rcdn 1
will remove them.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width ".Ev RCRC"
.It Ev RCRC
User configuration file. Defaults to
.Pa ~/.rcrc .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Pa ~/.rcrc
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl rcgc
.Dl rcgc -qf
.Dl rcgc -a
.Dl rcgc -d ~/corporate-dotfiles
.Sh SEE ALSO
